    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "idk man it's just super frustrating because we fix one thing and another thing breaks out of nowhere. it's almost like there's some kind of mischief happening in the codebase. how do we make sure we're actually catching everything and not just playing whack-a-mole with these weird little errors?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Whack-a-mole syndrome in development is a classic sign of interconnected, brittle code, which is very common in complex applications like fintech apps. To stop the constant breakage, you need to implement robust end-to-end (E2E) testing that simulates real user flows, not just isolated components. Think of it like this: instead of just testing a single button press, you need to test the entire journey—from logging in, to checking a balance, to making a transaction.\n\nThis thorough testing coverage acts as a safety net, preventing those \"out of nowhere\" errors from slipping through. It forces you to validate the *interactions* between components, which is where most of these subtle accessibility and rendering glitches hide. If you treat your tests like a highly vigilant guard, you'll catch the mischief before the user does.",
      "source": "target",
